   "passage": "**[1]** Coral reefs look like rock, but they are built by tiny animals called polyps. Inside each polyp live microscopic algae, and the two depend on each other. The algae produce food from sunlight and pass most of it to the coral; in return, they receive shelter. This arrangement is so vital that a coral without its algae is, in effect, starving.\n\n**[2]** When sea water becomes unusually warm, the partnership breaks down. The stressed coral expels the algae, and because they give the coral its colour, the reef turns white, a process known as bleaching. A bleached reef is not dead, however. If the water cools within a few weeks, the algae return and the coral recovers. Prolonged heat, on the other hand, is fatal.\n\n**[3]** Scientists are now searching for corals that are naturally resilient, that is, able to survive heat that kills their neighbours. Some hope to grow these tough varieties in nurseries and plant them on damaged reefs. Critics caution that this can only buy time: unless the oceans stop warming, even the hardiest coral will eventually reach its limit.",
